.TH IPHONE-MIRROR-BRIDGE 1 "2026" "iphone-mirror-bridge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iphone-mirror-bridge \- control an iPhone with a Linux touchpad or mouse over Bluetooth
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iphone-mirror-bridge
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Makes the computer act as a Bluetooth LE mouse for an iPhone. With
AssistiveTouch enabled on the iPhone, the built-in touchpad (or a mouse)
moves the iPhone pointer. Ctrl+F8 switches between the computer and the iPhone.
.PP
Normally this runs as the \fBiphone-mirror-bridge\fR systemd service, configured
in \fI/etc/iphone-mirror-bridge/bridge.conf\fR. Stop the service before running
the command by hand. It must run as root.
.SH TOUCHPAD GESTURES
One finger moves; tap or press clicks; two-finger tap or press right-clicks;
two fingers scroll; three fingers hold (long-press) and drag.
.SH OPTIONS
Run \fBiphone-mirror-bridge \-\-help\fR for the full list. Common options:
.TP
.B \-\-mouse
Use a mouse even when a touchpad exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " /dev/input/eventN"
Use a specific input device.
.TP
.BI \-\-touchpad\-speed " N"
Touchpad pointer speed (default 20).
.TP
.BI \-\-hotkey " KEY"
Key used with Ctrl to switch modes (default F8).
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Verbose logging.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/iphone-mirror-bridge/bridge.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR bluetoothctl (1)
